# Use one normalized portable mod import contract

Use one inspectable SHAR mod-package contract across desktop and mobile. A mod is
provided as either a directory or `.zip` containing its manifest, source and/or
assets; opaque precompiled payloads are not the preferred canonical format.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Accept one mod directory or one `.zip` package without requiring authors
  to rebuild the faithful base pipeline.
- [ ] On desktop, use the portable game installation's `mods/` directory as the
  normal drop location.
- [ ] On Android and iOS, define one exact app-owned external/user-visible mods
  location after final application identities and platform storage rules are
  fixed; publish those exact paths in README and in-app help.
- [ ] Keep the same logical mod identity, manifest schema, dependency rules,
  integrity checks, and load-order semantics across all supported platforms.
- [ ] Keep mod source/assets inspectable and editable. “Open” here means not
  intentionally locked or obfuscated; it does not impose GPL or any other
  license on third-party mod authors.
- [ ] Let each mod declare its own license/provenance and require distributors to
  have the rights needed for assets and code they redistribute.
- [ ] Warn users to trust the author/distributor they choose; hashes can prove an
  exact package matches a known hash, but cannot prove that unknown code is
  harmless.
- [ ] Keep generated official-language mods and converted legacy LMLM mods on
  this same package contract instead of inventing private special cases.
- [ ] Update README whenever desktop/mobile locations or package shape changes.

## Dependencies

Depends on deterministic mod identity and package validation contracts.

## Evidence

The previous task title mentioned desktop and Android only. The product contract
now explicitly includes iOS and macOS/Linux/Windows while preserving one logical
mod format.
